# XCU newgrp
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# newgrp starts a new shell, so the tests feed that shell commands on a pipe
# and read what it prints. The shell that runs is not named by the page, so
# only plain commands are fed to it. Password prompts, the group database and
# -l are not exercised. The group operand test names the group the process is
# already in, so a refusal cannot be told from success; the assertion allows
# either, as the page lets the new shell be created in both cases.

assertion  XCU.newgrp.001
anchor     utilities/newgrp.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/newgrp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       When newgrp creates the new shell, newgrp exits with the exit status
           of that shell.
test       t/001-shell-status.sh

assertion  XCU.newgrp.002
anchor     utilities/newgrp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       The new shell keeps the exported variables, the working directory and
           the file creation mask of the shell that ran newgrp.
test       t/002-environment-kept.sh

assertion  XCU.newgrp.003
anchor     utilities/newgrp.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/newgrp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       newgrp given a numeric group of the invoking process still creates the
           shell, and in it the real and effective group IDs are equal and are
           either that group or what they were before.
test       t/003-group-operand.sh

assertion  XCU.newgrp.004
anchor     utilities/newgrp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/newgrp.html#OPTIONS
class      untestable
says       Without an operand the group goes back to the user's entry; -l gives
           the environment of a new login; a password is read from the terminal
           when the group needs one and the user is not a member.
reason     The outcome depends on the group database and its passwords, which
           a test must not change, and the password is read from the terminal.
